ect  the only thing that remains constant is  his style of filming  every millimeter of movement makes a  huge difference  it's always about finding the right  place in the right shot  you also have to risk all the equipment  everything  you have to work out how it's going to  look with a skater coming towards me or  jumping over me  then pan towards the light  sometimes sebastian linda edits his  material right where he shot it so he  can upload a trailer version to the  internet there and then  i like sharing my films with the world  in the old days  you were limited to a particular tv  station or festival  and now there's this massive global  audience when you upload a film plus you  get feedback online  ivana  sebastian linda also uses the internet  to finance his films via crowdfunding  websites  he posts clips to keep his benefactors  up to date on how his projects are  progressing  for me it's also a tool for attracting  people to a project in advance and  encouraging them to get involved  so it's marketing as well as funding  funding  he's currently editing the follow-up to  his acclaimed revenge of the beasts  this time with 10 skaters out on the  streets of indonesia  the film also reveals how the locals  young and old alike reacted to a sport  largely unknown in their country  the great thing about my kind of films  is that you can just live your life with  the camera running  the good moments happen by themselves  it's not like going to work  if i look back on our trip  i'm not thinking about the camera  i have all those moments in my head and  i forget the camera  there are moments i've experienced  myself  you might not want to try these stunts  at home  but you can experience the onboard  thrills and spills from the safety of  your own screen  well in most german cities it's almost a  given that you will find a currywurst  stand it is germany's traditional  version of fast food but it's not just  popular with germans tourists like it so  much that startup companies realize  there was a demand for it outside of  germany nowadays you can find  traditional german sausages served in  many corners of europe and it's a trend  that looks definitely set to stay  this snack bar looks just like any other  in berlin or cologne but it's in central  warsaw  wurst kiosk as it's known specializes in  german sausages and it's a hit  it has a fine flavor  and it's very delicately spiced  perfectly grilled too  it tastes really good  i've never been to germany but these  seem like the real thing  it tastes great after work  i have one almost every day i really  like them  wherever german expats live you'll find  german sausage normally but the man  behind this sausage bar is polish  bernard roselle used to be an  advertising he developed a taste for the  meaty german treat on business trips he  opened his first stand just in time for  the euro 2012 soccer championship in  poland  i found a niche because no one was  selling  german sausages  here in warsaw  and actually polish people like sausages  grilled sausages especially during the  the summer so i thought to myself that  is a good starting point to open a small  small hole in the wall  roussel imports his product from just  across the border his snack bar or imbus  in german offers six varieties of  sausage meals and the top seller  currywurst a true berlin delicacy  had several meetings with various indies  in germany in germany and  i  just  watched how they are being prepared and  how they are served  with hot curry sauce and some you know  little things that are important for  german people and that's the way it goes  just like you know in berlin  about a million and a half tons of  sausage are produced in germany annually  about a fifth of that total gets  exported  to london for instance  the british capital boasts several  sausage stands one of the best known is  hermenza german  since opening three years ago it's made  a name for itself as the worst place to  go  the owners are german's florian frye and  aseda falakashi  they get their sausage straight from the  black forest from their neighborhood  butcher  they started out with just a catering  mobile  and then expanded to two restaurants  we never expected  we started out really small and went our  way and of course we're super happy that  things turned out the way they did  and in another corner of europe there  they are again at portugal's  southwestern tip you'll find the last  bratwurst stand before america  from spring till fall petra and wolfgang  bald sell their nurnberg style sausage  imported by air cargo  most of our customers are from spain but  of course we get lots of germans too  they've been satisfying customers for 17  years now  divorced kiosk in warsaw is branching  out now with some newsstands  and there are even mobile worst wagons  crisscrossing poland so what's the  secret  it's simple that it stays good  that's the way it is  any way you slice them smoke them fry  them or steam them come and get your  german sausages before someone else does  well lego man has seen many of britain's  sights and captured them all on his  camera in tandem photographer andrew  white has captured him capturing them  and this series of photos is proving a  big hit online playing with people's  perception of size and space is a  popular development in the world of  photography and andrew white does it all  with the mobile phone  the world viewed through the eyes of a  little lego person  the locations may be familiar but  captured from a pint-sized perspective  they look quite different  it's all part of a project by  photographer andrew white  the idea for the minifigure photography  came because we were on a family trip to  the lego store and my children found a  minifigure which had a camera accessory  and as a photographer myself i  immediately saw the potential of being  able to take this figure with me and  sort of tell a story from his point of  view looking at looking at him over the  shoulder as he goes around taking photos  white set out exploring the  possibilities and quickly decided to  abandon his regular camera in favor of a  smartphone  it has one major advantage  turned upside down the lens is almost at  ground level  literally within the first week of  having the figure i tried a couple of  shots of with my proper equipment um and  i found that i couldn't get close enough  to the figure and still have it in focus  and also the  the depth of field that i was achieving  meant that the the background was too  out of focus it just wasn't recognizable  many of the shots have been posted on  facebook but white also sells prints on  his website  when i'm looking for an opportunity to  take a photo then i'm looking usually  for either a well-known landmark like  we're in front of some paul's cathedral  today  or i'm looking for a scene that people  can relate to and i can portray in  perhaps a slightly different way  you know i've been able to capture  various different weather situations  they all make a a dramatic photo in  their own way  my favorite is  where the figure is sort of standing  looking at a rainbow but not actually  taking a photo he's just got his camera  down by his side um and it's kind of  reminding reminding us that you know  occasionally there's views that you need  to actually put the camera down and just  look and take it all in  andrew white isn't the only photographer  to work in miniature for the past few  years london-based artist sling katyu  has been creating small street scenes  populated by tiny people  he's published several volumes of  photographs and shown them in  exhibitions  two paris-based photographers who go by  the name of mini-miyam have also gotten  in on the act  their little people live on edible  landscapes and have exciting adventures  in germany tomas vreda sets toy models  in actual landscapes and takes close-ups  of them as though they were real  i'm certainly not the first person to  use small toys or lego in my photography  there's a lot of it  there's a lot of it happening there's a  lot of it predates what i'm doing um i  think what's what's different about what  i'm doing is doing it from the  perspective of a photographer so you're  actually understanding the story it's  very good to  to kind of contribute to a narrative and  to keep to keep things going from day to  day  he's dubbed his work legography  and also plans to put out a photo volume  soon  not too long ago andrew white decided to  give his lego person some company a crew  of tiny superheroes setting out to  conquer the world  be warned  want to learn more about europe and  european culture visit euromax on  facebook  you can tell us what you think and  you'll never miss an interesting topic  many euromax reports can also be looked  at again  there are behind the scenes looks at how  the reports and the program are  produced  we like it when fans visit euromax on  facebook  well a standard filter coffee can vary  widely in taste depending on where the  coffee bean comes from how they're  roasted and ground drinking coffee has  become about so much more than just a  caffeine fix here in europe and the  demand for a good brew is rising since  the year 2000 there has even been a  world cup for the best barista and this  year it was held in rimini italy having  already qualified in their own countries  54 internationals competed over four  days for the capital title  making coffee is a science in and of  itself  at the world barrista championship in  the italian resort of rimini everyone's  a pro they know their beans the right  roast  and how to get the grounds just fine  milk and water are also of the essence  everyone who brews up coffee has their  own recipe for the perfect cup  every second in your mouth the the mouth  feel and the flavor they change and give  you like a really movement and this is  what i'm looking in a blend how fine do  you want to grind your coffee or who how  course  so the grinder is like the main thing  it's a chain and every link has to fit  together from the producer right down to  the barista  during the four day competition each one  of the 54 coffee crazy contestants has  to serve the international jury  cappuccino espresso and a unique  beverage of their own called a signature  drink  the jury is specialized in analyzing the  complex aromas of the java served here  they've got certificates and years of  experience  many are world barista championship  veterans themselves  what you could see in the last couple of  years that baristas that are actually  competing and you know what ambassadors  for specialty coffee they interact much  more with the origin you know they know  much more and so as a judge you also  have to keep up you know just train  yourself and read a lot and travel a lot  just to keep up with the  knowledge all the competitors here are  gaining  anna toastbag is the german hopeful she  studied archaeology and first rose to  the coffee challenge helping out at a  cafe in munster  it inspired her to train to be a barista  for two and a half years she's been  leading the coffee school at the host  bar where she started out now she trains  new talent  her skills won her the german  championship last year  you can compare them and taste them and  notice wow that one's nutty the other  chocolatey and another is fruity that's  amazing there's always something new and  that just blew me away  her latest discovery is a pure  non-blended coffee from ethiopia the  homeland of the arabica bean  there she found beans she roasted in  various ways until she found just the  taste she wanted she's in the right  place at the right time germans are  suddenly raising the bar when it comes  to coffee  many people want to be able to taste  more aromas and they want to know  exactly where their coffee is from the  specific region  so interest in coffee has grown and  that's great  but there's still plenty of room for  improvement  scandinavians are the role models to  surpass in rimini  since the first championship in monaco  in 2000 scandinavians have won the title  six times  not surprising for the world's top  coffee consumers  dain sur and stila moccasin is at his  ninth championship  he says coffee is like a wine whose  flavors must be teased out and he says  the quality just keeps getting better  and better  i think it would be very interesting to  see  how  bars are set up with machines  because that's also changing this whole  line of you know industry where  express machines as you saw in old days  in italy with all the living machines i  mean that's coming back  so things just keep improving coffee has  now been claimed by the connoisseurs  and the experts at the competition in  italy are making sure their exacting  standards are met  and it was hidden izaki of japan who won  this year's world barista championship  so i'm sure all those customers are  fairly happy about that now once every  year in may the streets of monaco are  closed for the grand prix it's situated  on the french riviera and euromax went  along for the annual bash we did stray  from the track occasionally though just  to show you some of the other  attractions in the area  the race that goes through the center of  monaco starts on the busy albert the  first boulevard  german race car driver ellen law has  been living here for more than 20 years  and uses the boulevard daily  now and then i think here i am sitting  in the quiet neighborhood of la connamin  with a coffee  and a few years ago actually a bit  longer than that  50 meters from here i was holding the  trophy in my hands 50 meters  the peaceful quarter of la condamine is  located right alongside albert the first  boulevard  next to the palace cliffs known as the  rock la condemine is the oldest part of  monaco  it's now been made into a pedestrian  zone  is located right next to the starting  line  it's dominated by old buildings 