 room for  improvement  scandinavians are the role models to  surpass in rimini  since the first championship in monaco  in 2000 scandinavians have won the title  six times  not surprising for the world's top  coffee consumers  dain sur and stila moccasin is at his  ninth championship  he says coffee is like a wine whose  flavors must be teased out and he says  the quality just keeps getting better  and better  i think it would be very interesting to  see  how  bars are set up with machines  because that's also changing this whole  line of you know industry where  express machines as you saw in old days  in italy with all the living machines i  mean that's coming back  so things just keep improving coffee has  now been claimed by the connoisseurs  and the experts at the competition in  italy are making sure their exacting  standards are met  and it was hidden izaki of japan who won  this year's world barista championship  so i'm sure all those customers are  fairly happy about that now once every  year in may the streets of monaco are  closed for the grand prix it's situated  on the french riviera and euromax went  along for the annual bash we did stray  from the track occasionally though just  to show you some of the other  attractions in the area  the race that goes through the center of  monaco starts on the busy albert the  first boulevard  german race car driver ellen law has  been living here for more than 20 years  and uses the boulevard daily  now and then i think here i am sitting  in the quiet neighborhood of la connamin  with a coffee  and a few years ago actually a bit  longer than that  50 meters from here i was holding the  trophy in my hands 50 meters  the peaceful quarter of la condamine is  located right alongside albert the first  boulevard  next to the palace cliffs known as the  rock la condemine is the oldest part of  monaco  it's now been made into a pedestrian  zone  is located right next to the starting  line  it's dominated by old buildings and  apartments that don't have elevators  lokonomin is the opposite of hustle and  bustle  for me it's the most pleasant area here  there are plenty of nice shops far away  from the malls  you can shop and stroll too  many  we move on to the small church that  marks the spot where the start and  finish straightaways end  sant devote is where the course begins  winding its way up to the casino  monaco's most famous landmark  the place du casino glitters with a  glamour associated with the principality  international jet setting guests party  year round at the hotel de paris  monaco native ari doria has been  watching the goings on here for 70 years  it might not be a symbol of monaco but  it's a pretty typical site here the  plastic casino is where ferraris meet  and greet  this is where luxury gathers it's been  that way for a decade  the casino was the start of monaco's  wealth  from the place to casino the race course  heads back downhill towards the fairmont  a luxury hotel built in the middle of a  cliff above the sea  the best view of the tightest curve in  the grand prix can be had from the roof  terrace pool  it's something that former silver arrow  driver sterling moss enjoys he and his  wife susie are regulars here of course  they stay in the suite named after him  images of his monaco victories decorate  the walls the suite has a balcony  overlooking the water  it's in the right place  you've got a fantastic view and they go  past  and you know i think it's magic i mean  even even without bothering about the  grand prix it's sort of lovely you know  beautiful  dining room and everything oh i love it  yeah i'm not sure how i feel about you  looking down from the bedroom well i  figured  i'm picking up hints  i followed fans here and this thing i'm  moting him  after the hairpin turns around the  fairmont hotel there's a straight  stretch through the tunnel  but of course that doesn't mean you can  drive fast here  it's too bad but you have to obey the  traffic laws  legendary paris dakar rally winner yota  kleinschmidt is always happy to get back  into the light at the end of the tunnel  that's when there's a view of monaco's  yacht harbor por er cool  the ships that tie up here seem to get  bigger with each passing year  there are plenty of restaurants down at  port side particularly in the west  opposite the raskas curve which brings  the grand prix drivers back to the main  straight away  you miss a lot driving through here at  speed just like michael schumacher did  with his best time of 1 minute and 14  seconds  during the day you can have a coffee and  see more boats than you're likely to see  in many other places  and come evening it turns into a nice  promenade  in the bars and restaurants that line  the course the odds are pretty good at  any time of the year that you'll meet a  star race car driver because not  surprisingly many of them live in monaco  well thanks for watching that is it from  me and o'donnell and the rest of the  euromax crew for today if you miss  anything you can always catch up with us  online see you tomorrow bye  you
 

